// Three identical fingers (1.6 cm wide) with a small light behind each: how skin lets light through a thin part. Left: no subsurface scattering
// (a coated diffuse): the finger is a dark silhouette. Middle: the BSSRDF (Burley profile), which assumes a thick flat slab. Right: the finger filled
// with a scattering medium that has the same optical properties, and the light is followed through it by a random walk (docs/SKIN.md).
// Both use the scatter distance of skin, 3.67 / 1.37 / 0.68 mm (red / green / blue); the medium's albedo per channel is the single-scattering
// albedo that gives the same diffuse reflectance as the BSSRDF's albedo (0.80, 0.52, 0.42).
